{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"message_stop"},"session_id":"5775eff9-d29f-49ec-9e8e-059f81638978","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"52de9c01-cfe7-4897-9089-9f96ca05f752"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A1NoNioyToA28Dhm2dpyUV","type":"tool_result","content":"k8s-docs-control-plane-node-communication-2026-08-24.md:19:Kubernetes has a \"hub-and-spoke\" API pattern. All API usage from nodes (or the pods they run) terminates at the API server. None of the other control plane components are designed to expose remote services. The API server is configured to listen for remote connections on a secure HTTPS port (typically 443) with one or more forms of client authentication enabled. One or more forms of authorization should be enabled, especially if anonymous requests or service account tokens are allowed.\nk8s-docs-control-plane-node-communication-2026-08-24.md:47:Finally, Kubelet authentication and/or authorization should be enabled to secure the kubelet API.\nk8s-docs-cloud-native-security-2026-08-23.md:24:**Runtime protection: access.** The Kubernetes API is what makes your cluster work. Protecting this API is key to providing effective cluster security. Securing your cluster means implementing effective authentication and authorization for API access. Use ServiceAccounts to provide and manage security identities for workloads and cluster components. Kubernetes uses TLS to protect API traffic; make sure to deploy the cluster using TLS (including for traffic between nodes and the control plane) and protect the encryption keys.\nk8s-docs-pod-security-standards-2026-08-23.md:24:The standards are enforced by the built-in Pod Security Admission controller, which applies a policy per namespace via labels of the form pod-security.kubernetes.io/<MODE>: <LEVEL>, where MODE is enforce (violations are rejected), audit (violations are recorded in the audit log), or warn (violations trigger a user-facing warning), and LEVEL is privileged, baseline, or restricted.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5775eff9-d29f-49ec-9e8e-059f81638978","uuid":"c71c7dac-d17c-426f-ac4e-ae3b22308570","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:36:19.507Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"k8s-docs-control-plane-node-communication-2026-08-24.md:19:Kubernetes has a \"hub-and-spoke\" API pattern. All API usage from nodes (or the pods they run) terminates at the API server. None of the other control plane components are designed to expose remote services. The API server is configured to listen for remote connections on a secure HTTPS port (typically 443) with one or more forms of client authentication enabled. One or more forms of authorization should be enabled, especially if anonymous requests or service account tokens are allowed.\nk8s-docs-control-plane-node-communication-2026-08-24.md:47:Finally, Kubelet authentication and/or authorization should be enabled to secure the kubelet API.\nk8s-docs-cloud-native-security-2026-08-23.md:24:**Runtime protection: access.** The Kubernetes API is what makes your cluster work. Protecting this API is key to providing effective cluster security. Securing your cluster means implementing effective authentication and authorization for API access. Use ServiceAccounts to provide and manage security identities for workloads and cluster components. Kubernetes uses TLS to protect API traffic; make sure to deploy the cluster using TLS (including for traffic between nodes and the control plane) and protect the encryption keys.\nk8s-docs-pod-security-standards-2026-08-23.md:24:The standards are enforced by the built-in Pod Security Admission controller, which applies a policy per namespace via labels of the form pod-security.kubernetes.io/<MODE>: <LEVEL>, where MODE is enforce (violations are rejected), audit (violations are recorded in the audit log), or warn (violations trigger a user-facing warning), and LEVEL is privileged, baseline, or restricted.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
